# KS-678 spec-examples guard: rule E10, a URL example must carry a reserved example host (RUNG 3, code_patch2: two guard files edited, one in-place test hunk)

DRAFT by Spark operator o147 for Wednesday, 2026-11-12 AEDT (written ~02:3x from the files at develop 4f85ca04c622f29099d184bc45de2faa61fb4af0). Round 1 of 2 (original + ONE rebrief). Secuura (Platform K) only. A local PASS is a candidate, never a merge; the normal QA gate before merge. Origin: KS-678 (Backlog, 3 comments). The board seat's comment of 2026-11-09 NARROWED the ticket to its second ask: the 17 `secuura.io` URLs are gone from the spec (read by the board seat; the operator re-ran the guard on the tip's real spec: 0 findings), and what remains open is "a URL rule in the examples guard (an example whose value parses as a URL must have a host in a reserved set)", the ticket's own words. Exclusion census 2026-11-12 02:0x (88 open PRs, 336 files; 363 non-.pre READYs, 437 held paths read with both header forms): no open PR and no held READY names `rules-example.mjs` or `contract.mjs` of the spec-examples guard (PR 2092 touches `scripts/audit/baseline-contract.mjs`, a different file) or the ks256 test; Seat R 100th's rows touch none of them.

File: `Blockchain/Dev/frontend/verifier/src/components/VerifyPage.tsx`  (product, modified in place: 1 hunk, the E10 check inline in `sweepExample`)
File: `Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/contract.mjs`  (product, modified in place: 1 hunk, one rule-description row)
Test file: `Blockchain/Dev/packages/shared/src/__tests__/ks256-spec-example-contract.test.ts`  (EXISTING, modified in place: ONE hunk appending one `describe` block at the end of the file: 3 red cells, 2 control cells)
Tip: `4f85ca04c622f29099d184bc45de2faa61fb4af0` (origin develop; every line number and quote below was read from it)
Runner: `vitest` (`npx vitest run src/__tests__/ks256-spec-example-contract.test.ts` from `Blockchain/Dev/packages/shared`; the cells drive the real guard `node scripts/spec-examples/check-spec-examples.mjs --spec <temp file> --json`)
Tier: `code_patch2`
Rung: **3 (two product files, one in-place test hunk given byte-exact)**

## The mode - read this twice

CODE+TEST. Your diff touches EXACTLY 3 file(s), in this order:
1. `Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/rules-example.mjs` - modified in place: EXACTLY the 1 hunk under "The exact change", byte for byte.
2. `Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/contract.mjs` - modified in place: EXACTLY the 1 hunk under "The exact change", byte for byte.
3. `Blockchain/Dev/packages/shared/src/__tests__/ks256-spec-example-contract.test.ts` - modified in place: EXACTLY the 1 hunk under "The test", byte for byte.

You never touch any other file and no other line of the files above.

## What is wrong (one paragraph)

Rule E1 of the published-example guard reads email addresses only (it splits on `@`). A URL on any domain passes every one of E1 to E9, so `https://example.secuura.io/resource` (the KS-678 founding defect: a domain that does not resolve and that nobody is known to own) would be published again without a finding. The ticket asks for a rule that parses a URL-valued example and requires its host to be in a reserved set, or an allowlist entry with a reason. This brief adds that rule as E10: a string value that begins `http://` or `https://` and parses as a URL is a finding unless its host is `example.com`, `example.org`, `example.net` or `localhost`, or a subdomain of one of them (the real spec publishes `api.example.com` and `exports.example.com`; the operator ran the guard with this rule on the tip's real spec: 422 example blocks, 0 findings), or the existing allowlist excuses it for rule `E10` at that path (entries already need a reason of 10 characters).

## Where

- `Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/rules-example.mjs`: the `checkUsableCredential(...)` call inside `sweepExample` (line 54): the E10 block goes right after it, before the `return;`. It uses the `field` the other checks receive (so a finding on a scalar example names the property, not the literal key `example`), and `isAllowed` and `record`, both already imported.
- `Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/contract.mjs`: the `RULE_DESCRIPTIONS` table (line 306: the E9 row is its last).
- Untouched: every other rule, `allowlist.mjs`, `check-spec-examples.mjs`, the spec, the allowlist JSON. The reserved list is a local `const` inside the block, because the harness refuses a pure insertion at the end of a file and a new product file.

DESIGN CALLS (the operator's, for the raise PR to state): subdomains of a reserved name pass (the ticket's reserved set is "example.com/.org/.net, localhost" and the real spec uses `api.example.com`); only values that BEGIN with `http://` or `https://` are read (a URL inside prose is not); a URL that does not parse is left to the other rules; the existing nine-rule cell `fires every one of the nine rules on a planted leak` is NOT edited (its planted spec carries no URL, so it still reads nine); the ten-rule claim is carried by the new block.

## The exact change

2 hunks across 2 files, DICTATED: the file header lines and every `@@` header are copied character for character; do not recount. Context lines start with ONE space, removed lines with `-` (there are none), added lines with `+` (byte-exact; spaces only). Each product section has THREE header lines before its first `@@`: `diff --git a/<path> b/<path>`, `--- a/<path>`, `+++ b/<path>`.

```diff
diff --git a/Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/rules-example.mjs b/Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/rules-example.mjs
--- a/Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/rules-example.mjs
+++ b/Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/rules-example.mjs
@@ -53,4 +53,21 @@
     checkAccountId(node, docPath, allow, findings, field);
     checkUsableCredential(node, docPath, allow, findings, field);
+    // E10 (KS-678): a value that parses as an http(s) URL must carry a host RFC 2606 / RFC 6761 reserves (or `localhost`), or a subdomain of
+    // one. E1 reads addresses only, so a URL on any domain used to pass every rule; an allowlist entry with a reason excuses one example.
+    if (/^https?:\/\//i.test(node)) {
+      let host = '';
+      try {
+        host = new URL(node).hostname.toLowerCase();
+      } catch {
+        host = '';
+      }
+      const reserved = ['example.com', 'example.org', 'example.net', 'localhost'];
+      if (host && !reserved.some((name) => host === name || host.endsWith(`.${name}`)) && !isAllowed(allow, 'E10', docPath)) {
+        record(findings, 'E10', docPath, node,
+          `URL host "${host}" is not a reserved example host (example.com, example.org, example.net, localhost) — ` +
+          'a published URL on any other domain names a place anyone may register',
+          field);
+      }
+    }
     return;
   }
diff --git a/Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/contract.mjs b/Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/contract.mjs
--- a/Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/contract.mjs
+++ b/Blockchain/Dev/scripts/spec-examples/check/contract.mjs
@@ -305,2 +305,3 @@
   E9: 'usable-credential a request example pairing an email with a real password',
+  E10: 'url-host          a URL example whose host is not a reserved example host (KS-678)',
 };
```

**Do NOT touch** any other line of either file, any message text, or any other rule.

## The test

File: `Blockchain/Dev/packages/shared/src/__tests__/ks256-spec-example-contract.test.ts`

ONE in-place hunk appending a `describe` block with five cells (R1, R2, R3 red; C1, C3 control: the names below are the cells' own labels). The `+` lines are byte-exact: copy them. The block builds one-schema specs at run time in a temp file, never committed.

```diff
diff --git a/Blockchain/Dev/packages/shared/src/__tests__/ks256-spec-example-contract.test.ts b/Blockchain/Dev/packages/shared/src/__tests__/ks256-spec-example-contract.test.ts
--- a/Blockchain/Dev/packages/shared/src/__tests__/ks256-spec-example-contract.test.ts
+++ b/Blockchain/Dev/packages/shared/src/__tests__/ks256-spec-example-contract.test.ts
@@ -632,2 +632,91 @@
   });
 });
+
+describe('KS-678 — rule E10: a URL example must carry a reserved example host', () => {
+  /**
+   * E1 reads addresses only (it splits on `@`), so `https://example.secuura.io/resource` passed every rule while naming
+   * a domain that does not resolve and that anyone could register. E10 reads values that parse as an http(s) URL and
+   * accepts a host that is a reserved name (example.com, example.org, example.net, localhost) or a subdomain of one.
+   */
+  const guardPath = path.resolve(
+    path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)),
+    '../../../../scripts/spec-examples/check-spec-examples.mjs',
+  );
+
+  /** @returns the guard's JSON report for a spec written to a temp file. */
+  function runGuardOn(spec: string): { findings: { rule: string; field: string; value: string }[] } {
+    const specPath = path.join(fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'ks678-')), 'spec.yaml');
+    fs.writeFileSync(specPath, spec);
+    try {
+      const stdout = execFileSync('node', [guardPath, '--spec', specPath, '--json'], {
+        encoding: 'utf8',
+      });
+      return JSON.parse(stdout);
+    } catch (err) {
+      return JSON.parse((err as { stdout: string }).stdout);
+    } finally {
+      fs.rmSync(path.dirname(specPath), { recursive: true, force: true });
+    }
+  }
+
+  /** A one-schema spec whose `webhookUrl` property carries `example`. */
+  function urlSpec(example: string): string {
+    return [
+      'openapi: 3.0.3',
+      'info:',
+      '  title: KS-678',
+      'components:',
+      '  schemas:',
+      '    Hook:',
+      '      type: object',
+      '      properties:',
+      '        webhookUrl:',
+      '          type: string',
+      `          example: ${example}`,
+    ].join('\n');
+  }
+
+  it('RED ks678 R1 fires E10 on a URL whose host is not reserved, and names the field', () => {
+    const findings = runGuardOn(urlSpec('https://example.secuura.io/resource')).findings;
+    expect(findings.map((f) => f.rule)).toContain('E10');
+    expect(findings.find((f) => f.rule === 'E10')?.field).toBe('webhookUrl');
+  });
+
+  it('RED ks678 R2 fires E10 on a URL inside an object example too', () => {
+    const findings = runGuardOn(
+      [
+        'openapi: 3.0.3',
+        'info:',
+        '  title: KS-678',
+        'components:',
+        '  schemas:',
+        '    Hook:',
+        '      type: object',
+        '      example:',
+        '        successUrl: https://checkout.secuura.io/done',
+      ].join('\n'),
+    ).findings;
+    expect(findings.map((f) => f.rule)).toContain('E10');
+  });
+
+  it('control ks678 C1 stays silent on the reserved hosts and their subdomains', () => {
+    for (const url of [
+      'https://example.com/resource',
+      'https://api.example.com/v1',
+      'https://example.org/x',
+      'http://example.net:8080/x',
+      'http://localhost:6882/x',
+    ]) {
+      expect(runGuardOn(urlSpec(url)).findings.filter((f) => f.rule === 'E10')).toHaveLength(0);
+    }
+  });
+
+  it('RED ks678 R3 does not treat a host that merely ends in the reserved name as reserved', () => {
+    const findings = runGuardOn(urlSpec('https://notexample.com/resource')).findings;
+    expect(findings.map((f) => f.rule)).toContain('E10');
+  });
+
+  it('control ks678 C3 stays silent on a value that is not a URL', () => {
+    expect(runGuardOn(urlSpec('not-a-url')).findings.filter((f) => f.rule === 'E10')).toHaveLength(0);
+  });
+});
```

## Red cells
- RED ks678 R1
- RED ks678 R2
- RED ks678 R3

## Cells and controls

- RED at the tip, GREEN after: `R1` (a `webhookUrl` example `https://example.secuura.io/resource` gives an E10 finding naming the field), `R2` (a URL inside an object example), `R3` (`https://notexample.com/resource` is NOT treated as reserved: the match is the whole name or a `.`-prefixed suffix).
- GREEN at the tip and after: `C1` (the five reserved forms give no E10 finding; at the tip there is no E10 at all), `C3` (a non-URL value gives none).
- Every existing cell of the file stays green on both trees.

## Premises (each one measured, with where)

- Premise: the files read at `4f85ca04c622f29099d184bc45de2faa61fb4af0` (the operator's Spark cache clone, the same sha as `ls-remote origin develop` at 02:3x): `sweepExample` calls the four string checks at lines 51-54, `isAllowed`, `leafName` and `record` are imported at lines 18-19, `RULE_DESCRIPTIONS` ends with E9 at line 306.
- Premise: the operator applied the dictated change to a scratch copy of the guard and ran it on the tip's real `docs/openapi/secuura-api.yaml`: `exampleBlocks` 422, `findings` empty, exit 0 (the tip's own run: the same, so the rule adds no finding on the real spec); a planted `webhookUrl: https://example.secuura.io/resource` gives one E10 finding with field `webhookUrl` on the patched copy and none on the tip.
- Premise: the golden applies strictly at the tip (`git apply --check`); the harness `--control` run is the proof it is green.

## UNMEASURED - stated rather than glossed

- Whether any test or doc elsewhere enumerates the guard's rules (counts "nine") and now reads stale: only the ks256 cell named above was read.
- IPv6 or credentialed URLs (`http://user@host`), and a scheme other than http(s) (`ftp://`, `wss://`): not read by E10.
- Whether `https://localhost` should pass in the published spec (it does here, as the ticket's reserved set says).
- The ticket's two calls to the owner (do we own secuura.io; is example.com acceptable) are NOT answered here: `example.com` is simply the reserved set.

## Scope

The rule E10 in the guard, one description row, five test cells. Refs KS-678 (Refs, never closing words; the ticket's other ask is already moot per the board seat's comment).

## Output

Exactly ONE fenced ```diff block, nothing outside it: 3 file section(s) in the order of `## The mode`; every section starts `diff --git a/<path> b/<path>`. Every `+` line on its own physical line. Every context line keeps its leading space. Use the dictated `@@` headers unchanged.

